WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.577
A resolver receives Region East with a 90-second TTL
at time zero.

00:00:04.577 --> 00:00:09.640
At time 20, the authoritative policy switches new
answers to Region West.

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:16.160
Under the simplified ordinary-cache model, a lookup
at time 50 can still receive East from the

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:20.945
resolver’s cache; the answer becomes eligible for
refresh at time 90.

00:00:20.945 --> 00:00:27.301
Updating the authority changes future answers, not
every previously distributed copy at the same

00:00:27.301 --> 00:00:27.830
instant.

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:33.987
Existing connections add another layer: a client may
continue using an established connection

00:00:33.987 --> 00:00:36.635
without repeating DNS for every request.

00:00:36.635 --> 00:00:43.057
Multiple clients may also share one resolver, so a
single cached answer can direct a large group.

00:00:43.057 --> 00:00:48.629
Geographic steering often estimates location from
the resolver or partial client-network

00:00:48.629 --> 00:00:52.554
information, rather than knowing each user’s precise
position.

00:00:52.554 --> 00:00:56.163
Treat DNS as a discovery mechanism with caching
behavior.

00:00:56.163 --> 00:01:02.431
Combine it with regional load balancing and an
explicit data recovery strategy; changing a name-to-

00:01:02.431 --> 00:01:07.306
address answer does not by itself make another
region ready to accept writes.
